## Diwali

In every year with data, Diwali night rises far above the weather-and-fire baseline (Fig. 6). In the CPCB network median the excess ranged from {{ n.diwali_main_min|fmt }} to {{ n.diwali_main_max|fmt }} µg/m³ (mean {{ n.diwali_main_mean|fmt }}), against a 99th percentile of {{ n.diwali_placebo_p99|fmt }} µg/m³ across {{ n.diwali_placebo_n|fmt }} ordinary nights; {{ n.diwali_above_p99 }} of {{ n.diwali_main_n }} Diwali nights exceeded it (Fig. 7).

The firecracker rules made no visible difference. The mean excess was {{ n.diwali_permissive_mean|fmt }} µg/m³ in years with no effective restriction ({{ n.diwali_permissive_years|join(", ") }}), {{ n.diwali_green_window_mean|fmt }} µg/m³ in green-cracker years ({{ n.diwali_green_window_years|join(", ") }}) and {{ n.diwali_full_ban_mean|fmt }} µg/m³ in full-ban years with data ({{ n.diwali_full_ban_years|join(", ") }}){% if n.diwali_kruskal_p is not none %} (Kruskal–Wallis p = {{ n.diwali_kruskal_p|fmt(2) }}){% endif %}. The samples are small, but nothing suggests the bans shrank the spike. The largest excess in the record came in 2025, the first year green crackers were allowed again, which was also the year with the fewest farm fires.

Including the fire index in the baseline changed the US Embassy Diwali excess by a median of {{ n.fire_term_median_change|fmt(0) }} µg/m³, and after it is included the excess is no longer related to the fire exposure on Diwali night (Spearman ρ = {{ n.diwali_fire_rho_fire|fmt(2) }}, p = {{ n.diwali_fire_p_fire|fmt(2) }}, n = {{ n.cens_n }}). The festival's contribution is therefore not farm smoke being misattributed.

{% if n.fp and n.fp.ratio %}The Diwali evening carries a combustion fingerprint at CPCB Delhi sites (Fig. 10): the PM2.5/PM10 ratio rose in {{ n.fp.ratio.share_higher|fmt(0, pct=True) }} of {{ n.fp.ratio.n }} site-years (median {{ n.fp.ratio.median_ref|fmt(2) }} → {{ n.fp.ratio.median_diwali|fmt(2) }}, p = {{ n.fp.ratio.wilcoxon_p|fmt(3) }}){% if n.fp.so2 %}, SO₂ rose in {{ n.fp.so2.share_higher|fmt(0, pct=True) }} (median change {{ n.fp.so2.median_pct_change|fmt(0, sign=True) }}%, p = {{ n.fp.so2.wilcoxon_p|fmt(3) }}){% endif %}{% if n.fp.co %}, and CO rose in {{ n.fp.co.share_higher|fmt(0, pct=True) }}{% endif %}{% if n.fp.no2 %}, while NO₂, mostly from traffic, changed by a median of {{ n.fp.no2.median_pct_change|fmt(0, sign=True) }}%{% endif %}.{% endif %} The timing differs between cities (Fig. 9): the excess in Delhi builds from early evening to after midnight, while Chennai, where Deepavali is traditionally celebrated at dawn, shows a morning component.

