# Discussion

**Fire counts are losing their meaning as a target.** Official progress on stubble burning is reported in active-fire counts. Our results add two lines of evidence that these counts now understate burning: the night pass of the same satellite sees a growing share of fires, and each detected fire goes with a larger rise in Delhi's PM2.5. Reported declines of 80–90% in counts should not be read as declines of the same size in burning or in its effect on Delhi. Burnt-area mapping and geostationary sensors, which do not depend on a single overpass time, are better suited to tracking progress.

**The firecracker bans did not shrink Diwali night.** Across ten years, the weather- and fire-adjusted Diwali excess was no lower under a full ban than with green crackers or no effective restriction. This matches the Supreme Court's own observation in 2025 that air quality differed little between green-cracker and full-ban years [@dh2025], and points to enforcement rather than the wording of the rules.

**Monitoring fails when it matters most.** The monitor most widely used as Delhi's reference stops reporting during the hours of highest concentration on Diwali night. Studies and dashboards that average whatever hours survive will understate the festival. Reporting the number of missing hours alongside any Diwali figure, and using the network median rather than a single monitor, are simple remedies.

# Limitations

The CPCB archive gap from late 2022 to early 2025 removes three Diwalis and three burning seasons from the network record; for these years we rely on the US Embassy monitor, which is censored on Diwali night. The regime comparison rests on two to five Diwalis per regime and is descriptive. The fire index uses surface winds, while smoke travels at higher levels. Rural Punjab has few monitors, so the ground test uses urban ones, where local sources dominate the evening. Weather normalisation removes the effect of weather on average; it cannot remove every interaction between weather and emissions. Diwali dates and firecracker rules are compiled from secondary reports and should be checked against the original orders.

# Conclusions

Separating weather, farm smoke and fireworks with public data and open code is feasible, and the separation changes the story. Diwali adds a large, consistent excess that the firecracker bans have not reduced. The fall in fire counts overstates the fall in burning. And the reference monitor goes dark at the peak. We will score our Diwali 2026 forecast in public after the festival.

